<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gruffydd ap Gwenwynwyn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1286</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gruffydd ap Gwenwynwyn was prince of Powys Wenwynwyn in mid-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> in the 13th century. He alternated between alliance and conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-ap-gruffudd">Llywelyn ap Gruffudd</a> of Gwynedd, plotting against him in 1274, and supported Edward I in the conquest of Wales in 1282–1283. His son Owain gave up the princely title and held the lands as an English baron.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gruffydd-ap-gwenwynwyn.jpg" alt="Gruffydd ap Gwenwynwyn" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AlexD · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Powys.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gruffudd was the son of Gwenwynwyn and Margaret Corbet. He was still a child in 1216, when his father, driven from his lands by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-the-great">Llywelyn the Great</a>, died in exile. He grew up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, maintained by the king, and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> only after Llywelyn's death. When Dafydd ap Llywelyn was forced to come to terms with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-of-england">Henry III of England</a> in 1241, Gruffudd received most of his father's lands and paid homage to Henry for them. Around this time he married Hawise, daughter of John Lestrange of Knockin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shropshire">Shropshire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-ap-gruffudd">Llywelyn ap Gruffudd</a>'s power grew after 1255, Gruffudd stayed loyal to the English Crown and was driven into exile in 1257. In 1263, threatened with the loss of his lands, he went over to Llywelyn, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-montgomery">Treaty of Montgomery</a> confirmed this in 1267. In 1274 he, Hawise and their son Owain joined Llywelyn's brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dafydd-ap-gruffydd">Dafydd ap Gruffudd</a> in a plot to kill Llywelyn and make Dafydd prince of Gwynedd. Owain was to come with armed men on 2 February, but a snowstorm stopped him, and later that year he confessed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Bangor.</p><p>Summoned by Llywelyn, Gruffudd fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and raided Llywelyn's lands from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrewsbury">Shrewsbury</a>. After the war of 1277 he got his lands back, and a bitter dispute followed over Arwystli, which Llywelyn wanted settled by Welsh law and Gruffudd, backed by Edward I, by English law. Though now old, he fought for Edward in 1282; some have suggested that Owain was involved in Llywelyn's killing at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilmeri">Cilmeri</a> in December that year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the war of 1282–1283 the principality of Powys Wenwynwyn was abolished, and the family, now Marcher lords, took the surname de la Pole after their seat at Poole, the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/welshpool">Welshpool</a>. Edward I allowed them to build or rebuild <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/powis-castle">Powis Castle</a>. Owain increasingly ran the estate after 1283. Gruffudd was buried in the Blackfriars Priory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrewsbury">Shrewsbury</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gruffudd ap Gwenwynwyn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Gruffydd ap Gwenwynwyn</b> fought and allied with Llywelyn ap Gruffudd in turn, and plotted against him in 1274.</blockquote><a href="/state/powys-wenwynwyn">Powys Wenwynwyn</a></li></ul></section>
